import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The crypto package: installing it, a signed link as an app writes one, the modules, and what they
## are checked against. Static: the sample is server/test/app/Samples.roc's, which the server's
## tests run.
meta = {
	title: "Crypto",
	description: "The crypto package for Roc Solid apps: SHA-256, HMAC-SHA256, HKDF-SHA256, Base64 and hex, in plain Roc on no platform, each checked against its RFC's test vectors.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing" },
		{ id: "links", label: "A signed link" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "checked", label: "Checked against" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/crypto</code>]}
				title="Hashes, signatures and keys, in Roc"
			>
				SHA-256, HMAC, HKDF, Base64 and hex: what an app keeps a token's digest with, signs a link with, and
				derives one key from another with. Plain Roc on no platform, so the server, a page in the browser and
				an <code>expect</code> use the same code.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="install">Installing</Heading>
			<Code
				title="main.roc's header"
				source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\", crypto: \"../packages/crypto/main.roc\" }"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				Then <code>import crypto.Hmac</code>, <code>import crypto.Base64</code> and so on, where they are used.
				What needs the host stays on the server platform: <code>AesGcm</code> (encryption) and
				<code>Random</code> (bytes from the operating system); see <a href={Paths.platform_crypto}>the platform's
				crypto</a>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="links">A signed link</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_links.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_links.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The link-signing key is derived from the app's <code>APP_SECRET</code> with HKDF, for that use alone;
				what the link says is signed with HMAC-SHA256 and written in Base64's URL alphabet.
				<code>Hmac.verify_sha256</code> compares in constant time, so how long it takes doesn't tell a forger
				how much of a signature was right.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Sha256</code></td><td><code>hash</code>: 32 bytes; <code>hex</code>: 64 hex digits; <code>chunks</code>: the digest of pieces, without joining them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hmac</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, message)</code>; <code>verify_sha256(key, message, signature)</code> and <code>equal</code>, in constant time</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hkdf</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, salt, info, length)</code>, <code>TooLong</code> past 8160 bytes; its two steps, <code>extract</code> and <code>expand</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Base64</code></td><td><code>encode</code>, <code>decode</code>; <code>url_encode</code> (unpadded), <code>url_decode</code>; <code>encode_unpadded</code>, <code>url_encode_padded</code>; <code>lines</code> (MIME's 76-character lines)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hex</code></td><td><code>encode</code> (lowercase), <code>decode</code> (either case)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Decoding is strict, so one text means one value: only the alphabet asked for, no spaces or line
				breaks, padding left out or exactly right, and the unused bits of the last character zero.
			</p>

			<Heading id="checked">Checked against</Heading>
			<p>
				Each module's <code>expect</code>s hold it to its published vectors: NIST's SHA-256 examples, every test
				case of RFC 4231 (HMAC), RFC 5869's SHA-256 cases (HKDF), and RFC 4648's (Base64, hex).
				<code>Sha256</code> wraps Roc's builtin <code>Crypto.SHA256</code>. The server platform's tests also
				check each against Node's <code>crypto</code>, from a running server.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
